Backstory/Pretext to add for infection: The outbreak began in rural Nebraska, where an unfamiliar fungus spread through corn used as cattle feed. The organism survived digestion and established itself in the animals’ nervous and glandular tissues. Infected cattle showed no obvious symptoms, allowing them to pass inspection before their meat entered the national food supply.
Humans and household pets were initially exposed by eating contaminated beef or handling it during preparation. Many animals became asymptomatic carriers, harboring the infection in their saliva without developing the neurological illness seen in humans. Once the organism adapted to its new hosts, bites and other contact with infected saliva became the primary means of transmission.
For several days, infected people appeared healthy while the fungus spread along their peripheral nerves and into the brain. The first symptoms—fever, confusion, agitation, and loss of impulse control—were easily mistaken for an acute illness or drug-induced psychosis. The final stage brought catastrophic neurological damage: victims became violently aggressive, largely unresponsive to pain, and driven to attack anyone nearby.
